STM32F407
=========

A Cortex-M4 with 1M of flash, 128K of SRAM at 0x20000000 and 64K of
core coupled memory at 0x10000000, and a floating point extension that
is single precision only.  The same part family as the NUCLEO-F303RE
beside it, with four times the RAM and eight times the flash, and that
is the whole difference that matters: this board runs Limbo and that
one does not.

What was measured
-----------------

Under qemu, which models an STM32F405 as -M netduinoplus2 -- the same
die family, and the same CCM, SRAM and flash geometry:

	qemu-system-arm -M netduinoplus2 -nographic -kernel istm32f407

qemu gives serial_hd(0) to USART1, which is this board's console, so
-nographic connects it and nothing else is needed.  A kernel built with
CONSUART at 2 wants -serial null -serial mon:stdio instead; with a
plain -nographic its console is attached to a port the kernel never
writes to and the board looks dead.

What that run does, typed at the prompt and answered:

	; ls /dis
	/dis/bind.dis
	/dis/cat.dis
	/dis/echo.dis
	/dis/lib
	/dis/ls.dis
	/dis/sh
	/dis/sh.dis
	; cat /dev/time
	         95           95356402154            1525862433              16000000
	; echo hello world
	hello world

and the FPREG, FPREG-S2 and TRAPREG checks the armv7m kernel prints at
boot all pass.

Where the memory went
---------------------

163840 bytes are left after the kernel.  Loading /dis/sh.dis asks
malloc for 73536 bytes for its decoded instruction array alone -- 6128
instructions at 12 bytes an Inst -- and on this board that is not an
optimisation question, it is the difference between having a shell and
not.  The mkfile beside this file sets XIPMODS=1, so utils/dis2s lays
every module's Inst array out at build time as a RODATA symbol in the
text segment, and the array costs flash instead of RAM.  The kernel
image is 537816 bytes of the megabyte.

Getting from 147456 bytes free to 163840 took four portable sizes that
were chosen for a machine with megabytes.  conf.base0 is PGROUND(end),
so the kernel's data and bss are charged to the pools in whole 4096-
byte pages, and each of these was measured from the linker's symbol
table:

	mntalloc.tagmask	8192	a bit for each of the 65536 tags
				9P can express (NMNTTAG, 1024 here)
	Uart.istage/ostage	4096	2048 each way in every Uart
				(STAGESIZE, 256 here)
	xlists.hole		2240	128 free-memory runs; this board
				has two banks (NHOLE, 16 here)
	kmesg			1536	the console log (KMESGSIZE)
	ilockpcs		1024	a debug ring nothing writes
				unless LOCKCYCLES is defined

Data and bss went from 48736 bytes to 32280, which is nine pages to
eight.  The last of those was worth a whole page on its own: at
KMESGSIZE 1024 the kernel ended 24 bytes into the ninth page.

What is not done
----------------

ls(1) runs on a freshly booted board and does not run after another
command has run first.  This is not a bug with a fix in this
directory.  The largest single thing the Dis heap is asked for is the
8192-byte buffer bufio(2) makes for each open file, and whether one
exists depends on where the previous command left its holes; at the
prompt main holds about 70K and the heap about 50K of the 163840, and
the failure is honest -- "out of memory: heap" is raised into the
shell, which prints it and carries on.  The pool percentages in the
configuration beside this file were chosen by measurement for that
behaviour and the comment there says what the alternatives did.

Nothing here has been run on silicon.

A probe is not needed to put it there.  Every STM32F4 has a bootloader
in ROM that speaks a serial protocol on USART1 -- PA9 and PA10 -- and
is entered by holding BOOT0 high over a reset, so two wires and a USB
serial adapter both write the kernel and carry its shell:

	# BOOT0 high, then reset
	stm32flash -w istm32f407 -v -g 0x08000000 /dev/cu.SERIALPORT
	# BOOT0 back to ground, then reset
	screen /dev/cu.SERIALPORT 115200

The linker's -H4 output is a raw image whose first two words are the
initial stack pointer and the reset vector -- 0x200074fc and
0x080001c1, which is why qemu's -kernel takes it -- and that is exactly
what the ROM bootloader wants written at 0x08000000.  That is the
reason CONSUART in mem.h is USART1 and not the USART2 an ST-Link
virtual port would use: the console is on the pins that flash the
board, so the wire does not have to move.  There is no semihosting
console in this tree, so the UART is not optional either way: without
it a booted kernel has nowhere to say anything.

A probe would be the other way, and the one here does not reach this
part.  The board is on the end of an Olimex ARM-USB-TINY-H, and openocd
reaches a DAP -- JTAG tap 0x4ba00477, and the debug and system power-up
handshakes in DP CTRL/STAT both acknowledged, 0xf0000001 -- but the
only access port on it reads IDR 0x24770002, which is an APB-AP.  An
STM32F4 answers 0x24770011, an AHB-AP, at the same index, and openocd
cannot read 0xe000ed00 through what is there.  Whatever is on the end
of that cable is not answering as an STM32F4.  With a probe that does:

	openocd -f interface/ftdi/olimex-arm-usb-tiny-h.cfg \
		-f target/stm32f4x.cfg \
		-c "program istm32f407 0x08000000 verify reset exit"

What is here
------------

USART1 is on APB2 and USART2 on APB1, which is not a detail: the baud
divisor comes from whichever, and hsionly() in archstm32f407.c puts
both APB dividers back to one when the PLL does not lock.  Without
that, a fallback left HCLK at 16MHz with APB1 divided by four and APB2
by two -- because CFGR has to be written before the PLL is known to
have locked -- and the console would have come up at a fraction of the
baud it meant.

archstm32f407.c takes the clock tree as far as the part will let it and
no further.  setclock() tries HSI, then the PLL, and waits for each
ready bit with a bounded spin rather than forever, because qemu's
hw/misc/stm32_rcc.c is a plain register file that never sets HSIRDY or
PLLRDY: a kernel that waited for them would hang before its first
print.  It falls back to the 16MHz internal oscillator and sets apb1hz
to whatever it settled on, and uartstm32f4.c takes its divisor from
that, so a board whose PLL does not start has a console at the right
baud rather than at a tenth of it.  On silicon that fallback has never
been exercised either way.

uartstm32f4.c is a second file rather than an argument to
nucleof303/uartstm32.c because the F3 and the F4 share a name and a
manufacturer and not a register map: SR and DR here against the F3's
ISR, RDR and TDR, the enable bit at 13 rather than 0, and an overrun
cleared by reading SR and then DR rather than through an ICR this part
does not have.

UART0IRQ is the NVIC interrupt number -- 37 for USART1, 38 for USART2
-- and not that minus 16.  os/inferno/
armv7m/trap.c dispatches on IPSR minus Excirq and intrenable() uses its
argument as an NVIC ISER bit, so both want the number RM0090 calls
position 37 or 38.  With the -16 the port transmitted and never
received a character.  nucleof303/mem.h had the same -16 and now has
38, which is its USART2; that
board has no userland to type at, so the correction there is by reading
and not by measurement.
